INT. ABANDONED SCHOOLROOM, YORKSHIRE - DUSK (1851)

A single desk. A cracked slate. Snow drifts through a broken window and settles on a pile of primers.

The door creaks. MISS EDITH WHITLOCK, thirty, in a wool coat and bonnet, a trunk dragging behind her, steps in and stops.

She sets down the trunk. She crosses to the desk. She wipes frost from it with her glove and takes a folded letter from her sleeve.

EDITH (V.O.)
(reading, her own voice)
"Dear Miss Whitlock. We regret the schoolhouse at Thornby Moor has been closed these four years."

She turns to the slate. A single word is chalked there in a child's hand: STAY.

EDITH (V.O.) (CONT'D)
"The parish cannot afford a mistress. We trust you will find other employment."

She sets the letter down. She takes off her bonnet. She picks up a stub of chalk, and under STAY she writes, very slowly, MONDAY.
